---
import CaseStudy from "../layouts/CaseStudy.astro";
import CaseFolder from "../components/CaseFolder.astro";
// Case study: PeARS, from its copy in Notion ("Pears case study"), laid out
// as On My Disk's. The before & after screenshots are Figma's (Studies).
---
<CaseStudy title="PeARS — making contributing faster & simpler — Anna Volkova" description="PeARS: making contributing to an open-source search engine faster & simpler. A case study by Anna Volkova, product & visual designer."
  contents={[
    { id: "overview", label: "Overview" },
    { id: "impact", label: "Impact" },
    { id: "role", label: "My Role" },
    { id: "decisions", label: "Key Design Decisions" },
    { id: "outcome", label: "Outcome" },
    { id: "see-more", label: "See more" },
  ]}
  screen={{ src: "assets/img/work/pears-screen.webp", alt: "PeARS’ search page: a search for “AI”, with a list of results.", width: 1068, height: 633 }}>
    <h1 class="cs-title cs-block">Making contributing to an open-source search engine faster &amp; simpler</h1>

    <div class="cs-intro">
      <dl class="cs-details">
        <div class="cs-block"><dt>Role</dt><dd>UX consultant<br>Product designer</dd></div>
        <div class="cs-block"><dt>Team</dt><dd>1 designer<br>1 engineer</dd></div>
        <div class="cs-block"><dt>Skills</dt><dd>Product, UX</dd></div>
        <div class="cs-block"><dt>Timeline</dt><dd>Jan–Mar 2024</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <section class="cs-overview" aria-labelledby="overview">
        <h2 class="cs-label cs-block" id="overview">Overview</h2>
        <div class="cs-overview-text">
          <p class="cs-block">PeARS is an open-source, privacy-focused search engine designed for researchers and non-profits. Because it relies on users manually submitting web pages to their search engine instances, the challenge was to remove usability barriers that made contributing difficult.</p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>

    <h2 class="cs-divider cs-block" id="impact"><span>Impact</span></h2>

    <div class="cs-stack">
      <p class="cs-text cs-block">All of my design recommendations were shipped. They led to better accessibility — color contrast and touch target size issues were eliminated &amp; night mode implemented. The time needed to submit an entry was reduced by more than half.</p>
    </div>

    <section class="cs-role" aria-labelledby="role">
      <h2 class="cs-label cs-block" id="role">My Role</h2>
      <p class="cs-text cs-block">I worked as an independent UX consultant with the PeARS team over seven weeks, from January to March 2024. I conducted a competitive analysis, UX audit, and usability testing, then delivered wireframes and recommendations covering accessibility, navigation, terminology, and system feedback.</p>
    </section>

    <h2 class="cs-divider cs-block" id="decisions"><span>Key Design Decisions</span></h2>

    <!-- Each decision: what and why, on the right or the left, then its
         before & after on paper. -->
    <div class="cs-solutions">
      <section class="cs-solution cs-solution--right" aria-labelledby="decision-terms">
        <div class="cs-solution-text">
          <h3 class="cs-solution-title cs-block" id="decision-terms">Replacing technical terminology with familiar language</h3>
          <p class="cs-solution-desc cs-block">Terms such as “Indexer” and “Pods” were unfamiliar to the core user base of researchers, making it difficult to understand how to submit a website and choose a category. I recommended replacing system-oriented terminology with more familiar language to make these actions easier to understand — “suggest” and “categories”.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="cs-frame cs-frame--fit cs-paper cs-block cs-block--media">
          <img class="cs-paper-grain" src="assets/img/on-my-disk/card-paper.webp" alt="" width="429" height="625">
          <div class="cs-row cs-row--top">
            <figure class="cs-figure" style="--r: 1;">
              <figcaption class="cs-caption">Before</figcaption>
              <div class="cs-shot"><img loading="lazy" src="assets/img/pears/pods.webp" alt="Before: a pod, “Pod: design”, with its 6 entries and a View button." width="570" height="351"></div>
            </figure>
            <figure class="cs-figure" style="--r: 1;">
              <figcaption class="cs-caption">After</figcaption>
              <div class="cs-shot"><img loading="lazy" src="assets/img/pears/categories.webp" alt="After: the Categories page, listing Machine learning, Big Tech and People with their entries." width="1440" height="1282"></div>
            </figure>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="cs-solution" aria-labelledby="decision-actions">
        <div class="cs-solution-text">
          <h3 class="cs-solution-title cs-block" id="decision-actions">Making contribution actions clearer</h3>
          <p class="cs-solution-desc cs-block">Users needed clearer ways to contribute web pages to their search engine instances. I recommended more prominent calls to action to make submission options easier to identify. Example — if a query search yields no results, the page shows a “Contribute” button.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="cs-frame cs-frame--fit cs-paper cs-block cs-block--media">
          <img class="cs-paper-grain" src="assets/img/on-my-disk/card-paper.webp" alt="" width="429" height="625">
          <div class="cs-row cs-row--top">
            <figure class="cs-figure" style="--r: 1;">
              <figcaption class="cs-caption">Before</figcaption>
              <div class="cs-shot"><img loading="lazy" src="assets/img/pears/home-before.webp" alt="Before: the home page, a search bar and a note that every other function has been disabled, with Indexer and Pods in the menu." width="1231" height="855"></div>
            </figure>
            <figure class="cs-figure" style="--r: 1;">
              <figcaption class="cs-caption">After</figcaption>
              <div class="cs-shot"><img loading="lazy" src="assets/img/pears/search-results.webp" alt="After: search results with an “Add a resource” button below them, and Add a resource in the menu." width="1440" height="832"></div>
              <div class="cs-shot"><img loading="lazy" src="assets/img/pears/add-resource.webp" alt="After: the Add a resource page, with the page’s URL, a category and an optional trigger warning." width="1440" height="838"></div>
            </figure>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="cs-solution cs-solution--right" aria-labelledby="decision-consistency">
        <div class="cs-solution-text">
          <h3 class="cs-solution-title cs-block" id="decision-consistency">Improving consistency, accessibility, and system feedback</h3>
          <p class="cs-solution-desc cs-block">Usability testing revealed inconsistent navigation patterns between pages and a lack of feedback during errors, both of which led to confusion and decreased confidence. I recommended consistent navigation across pages, clearer error feedback, improved color contrast, larger touch targets, and a night mode.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="cs-frame cs-frame--fit cs-paper cs-block cs-block--media">
          <img class="cs-paper-grain" src="assets/img/on-my-disk/card-paper.webp" alt="" width="429" height="625">
          <div class="cs-row cs-row--top">
            <figure class="cs-figure" style="--r: 1;">
              <figcaption class="cs-caption">Before</figcaption>
              <div class="cs-shot"><img loading="lazy" src="assets/img/pears/nav-before.webp" alt="Before: a blue menu bar with Acknowledgments, GitHub, Twitter and Logout." width="638" height="392"></div>
              <div class="cs-shot"><img loading="lazy" src="assets/img/pears/form-before.webp" alt="Before: the old form’s fields, the browser’s inspector measuring one of its targets at 31 by 38 pixels." width="703" height="331"></div>
            </figure>
            <figure class="cs-figure" style="--r: 1;">
              <figcaption class="cs-caption">After</figcaption>
              <div class="cs-shot"><img loading="lazy" src="assets/img/pears/home-after.webp" alt="After: the home page, with the same menu and footer as every other page." width="1440" height="1272"></div>
              <div class="cs-shot"><img loading="lazy" src="assets/img/pears/login.webp" alt="After: the login form, telling you it couldn’t find an account with that email address." width="1692" height="985"></div>
            </figure>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="cs-frame cs-frame--fit cs-paper cs-block cs-block--media">
          <img class="cs-paper-grain" src="assets/img/on-my-disk/card-paper.webp" alt="" width="429" height="625">
          <div class="cs-row">
            <div class="cs-shot" style="--r: 0.459;"><img loading="lazy" src="assets/img/pears/phone-home.webp" alt="After, on a phone: the home page." width="804" height="1752"></div>
            <div class="cs-shot" style="--r: 0.528;"><img loading="lazy" src="assets/img/pears/phone-search.webp" alt="After, on a phone: search results." width="809" height="1533"></div>
            <div class="cs-shot" style="--r: 0.528;"><img loading="lazy" src="assets/img/pears/phone-add-resource.webp" alt="After, on a phone: the Add a resource page." width="809" height="1533"></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>

    <h2 class="cs-divider cs-block" id="outcome"><span>Outcome</span></h2>

    <!-- What came of it, side by side, edged as the decisions are. -->
    <ul class="cs-next cs-next--three">
      <li class="cs-solution-text"><p class="cs-solution-desc cs-block">All recommended design changes, including terminology change, consistent navigation and night mode were shipped.</p></li>
      <li class="cs-solution-text"><p class="cs-solution-desc cs-block">Accessibility issues including color contrast and touch-target sizes were corrected.</p></li>
      <li class="cs-solution-text"><p class="cs-solution-desc cs-block">Submission time decreased from 38 seconds before the redesign to 17 seconds after implementation, a 55% reduction. Both tests involved one participant already familiar with PeARS, so the result is indicative rather than conclusive.</p></li>
    </ul>

    <CaseFolder current="pears" />
</CaseStudy>
